The attached image (Figure 1) visually represents the three-step chemical synthesis of Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, from its precursor, phytoglycogen (PG). The figure shows PG reacting with octenyl succinic anhydride (OSA) to form phytoglycogen octenyl succinate (PG-OS), followed by a reaction with (3-chloro-2-hydroxypropyl)-trimethylammonium chloride (CHPTAC) to yield the final product, PG-OS-CHPTAC (Nano-11). These modifications introduce amphiphilic properties and a positive surface charge to Nano-11, which are essential for its function as a vaccine adjuvant, enabling efficient adsorption of negatively charged protein antigens.